The {{nihongo|'''Siege of Ichijōdani'''|一乗谷の役|Ichijōdani no Eki|extra=or, more commonly, 一乗谷の陣 ''Ichijōdani no Jin''}} was a series of battles undertaken by the [[Honda shogunate]] against the [[Ii clan]], and ending with the deaths of the leaders of both clans as well as the so-called "Great Reconciliation". The siege put an end to the last major armed opposition to the shogunate's establishment. 

==Outset==
Following the assassination of [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] (Honda Shigetada) during the [[Kinzō Incident]] on June 6, 1625, the grieving Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]] mobilized a massive punitive force. Despite the advice of his generals to wait for heavy artillery, Shigetomo ordered an immediate march on the Ii stronghold. The Shogunate forces, numbering over 70,000, converged on the narrow valley of Ichijōdani, where [[Ii Naotaka]] had dug in with fewer than 7,000 men.

The Ii clan, aware of their numerical disadvantage, destroyed the bridges across the Ichijōdani River and fortified the western banks, hoping to use the torrential monsoon rains to stall the Honda advance.

==Battle of the Ichijōdani Rapids==
Hostilities commenced on the morning of June 12. Honda vanguard commander Manabe Hachisuke attempted to ford the swollen river under cover of fog. However, the Ii musketeers, commanded by Kimura Shigetora, had pre-sighted the crossing points. When the fog lifted, the Honda vanguard was exposed to withering enfilade fire.

Manabe Hachisuke was killed in the first volley, and the Honda advance collapsed in confusion. Shigetomo, observing from Mount Hei, reportedly executed two of his own captains for cowardice, further damaging the morale of the Shogunate army. This initial engagement proved that despite the numbers, the Ii defenses were formidable.

==Battle of Nishi-ku==
By June 14, the rains had subsided, allowing the main Honda force under [[Hotta Muneshige]] to construct pontoon bridges. The Shogunate army crossed the river in force, overwhelming the Ii outer defenses through sheer weight of numbers. The fighting moved into Nishi-ku (West District), the merchant quarter outside the main castle walls.

The battle descended into brutal urban combat. Ii Naotaka ordered his troops to fight for every house to buy time for civilians to evacuate into the castle keep. The fighting lasted for two days. On June 16, the Ii general Kimura Shigetora launched a suicide charge to halt the advance of the Okudaira clan, allowing the remaining Ii forces to retreat behind the castle walls. Shigetora was killed, but his sacrifice stabilized the Ii defensive line.

==Castle Siege==
By June 17, [[Ichijōdani Castle]] was completely surrounded. Shigetomo established his command post just outside the main gate, well within the range of Ii cannons, displaying a reckless disregard for his own safety. He ordered a general assault at noon, but his second-in-command, Hotta Muneshige, fearing a massacre, held back the reserves. The assault failed, leaving thousands of Honda dead in the mud.

That night, a tense stalemate settled over the valley. Inside the castle, Ii Naotaka contemplated suicide to spare his men, while outside, Shigetomo descended into an alcohol-fueled psychosis, hallucinating the ghost of his father.

=="Great Reconciliation"==
The "Great Reconciliation" ({{nihongo|Dai-Wakai|大和解}}) refers to the events of the morning of June 18, 1625. It is considered one of the most pivotal and tragic moments in the history of Okaiken, marking the emotional end of the Sengoku mindset.

===Accepted historical position===
For centuries, official Shogunate records stated that Honda Shigetomo approached the castle to negotiate a surrender. While attempting to exchange terms, a "stray shot" from an undisciplined rebel soldier struck the Shogun. Ii Naotaka, witnessing the dishonorable act, rushed out to apologize. He then committed ''seppuku'' to atone for his subordinate's crime, and Shigetomo succumbed to his wounds shortly after. This version paints both men as victims of circumstance.

===Events from the ''Mitsukoji''===
The [[Mitsukoji]] chronicle, fully declassified in 2016, reveals a more personal tragedy. It states that Shigetomo rode out alone, wearing full armor, screaming for his childhood friend "Manchiyo" (Naotaka) to face him. He intended to provoke a duel but secretly carried a letter of forgiveness.

When Naotaka refused to fight, Shigetomo reached into his armor for the letter. An Ii sniper, mistaking the movement for a weapon, fired. Shigetomo was mortally wounded. Naotaka rushed to his side, and upon reading the bloodstained letter offering him the Bungo domain, realized the war was a misunderstanding. In a pact of brotherhood, Naotaka committed ''seppuku'' beside the dying Shogun so they could "sleep in the same earth."

===Honda clan witnesses===
[[Hotta Muneshige]] and the monk [[Sessai Taigen]] were the first to reach the bodies. Muneshige recorded that Shigetomo's final words were an order to "Seal it" ({{nihongo|Fūjiro|封じろ}}), referring to the cycle of vengeance. Muneshige acted as the second for Naotaka's suicide, honoring the Shogun's wish that the Ii lord die as a samurai, not a rebel.

===Ii clan witnesses===
[[Ii Naoyoshi]], Naotaka's son, watched from the ramparts. He later testified that his father threw away his sword before running to Shigetomo. Naoyoshi preserved the bloodstained letter, which became the Ii clan's most sacred treasure, proving that the Honda Shogun had forgiven them before his death.

==Aftermath==
The deaths of both leaders shocked the armies into an immediate ceasefire. Hotta Muneshige and Honda Shigetoki negotiated with Ii Naoyoshi.
* Amnesty: The Ii clan was spared annihilation. They surrendered the northern strategic half of Ichijōdani but retained the southern half.
* Succession: [[Honda Shigetoki]] became the 3rd Shogun.
* Political Shift: The tragedy effectively ended the resistance of the ''tozama'' daimyos, who were moved by the story of the "Double Suicide." It solidified the legitimacy of the Honda Shogunate not through force, but through shared grief.

==In popular culture==
The siege is the climax of the 1983 [[Taisen drama]] ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]''. The finale, depicting the "Great Reconciliation," achieved a 90.4% rating. The event is also commemorated annually in the Ichijōdani "Red String Festival."

